      "page_idx": 14,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "header",
          "bbox": [
            0.597,
            0.071,
            0.812,
            0.088
          ],
          "content": "Two Variables, One Line"
        },
        {
          "type": "page_number",
          "bbox": [
            0.853,
            0.071,
            0.88,
            0.086
          ],
          "content": "99"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.113,
            0.11,
            0.882,
            0.195
          ],
          "content": "But why does ${ } ^ { \\circ } m ^ { \\flat }$ represent the slope? To understand this let us consider two points $\\mathrm { A } ( x _ { 1 } , y _ { 1 } )$ and $\\mathrm { B }   ( x _ { _ 2 } ,   y _ { _ 2 } )$ which lie on the line $y = m x + d .$ Since they satisfy the equation we get, $y_{1} = mx_{1} + d$ and $y_{2} = mx_{2} + d.$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.152,
            0.197,
            0.83,
            0.217
          ],
          "content": "Now, the slope of the line passing through the points A and B is"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.196,
            0.218,
            0.798,
            0.258
          ],
          "content": "\\frac{y_{2}-y_{1}}{X_{2}-X_{1}}=\\frac{(mx_{2}+d)-(mx_{1}+d)}{X_{2}-X_{1}}=\\frac{mx_{2}-mx_{1}}{X_{2}-X_{1}}=\\frac{m(x_{2}-x_{1})}{x_{2}-x_{1}}=m",
          "image_source": "images/page_14_equation_4.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.116,
            0.26,
            0.882,
            0.372
          ],
          "content": "This is the reason why we can just read off the slope of a line if its equation is in the form $y = m x + d .$ This also shows that the slope m of a line with equation $y = m x + d$ is given by $\\frac { y _ { _ 2 } - y _ { _ 1 } } { X _ { _ 2 } - X _ { _ 1 } }$ for any two points $( x _ { 1 } , y _ { 1 } )$ and $( x _ { \\mathrm { { 2 } } } , y _ { \\mathrm { { 2 } } } )$ on the line, regardless of which points they are."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.116,
            0.374,
            0.881,
            0.413
          ],
          "content": "Note that the standard form of the equation of a line $ax + by + c = 0$ can be rewritten as"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.308,
            0.417,
            0.691,
            0.454
          ],
          "content": "by = -ax - c   or   y = -\\frac{a}{b}x - \\frac{c}{b}, (b \\neq 0)",
          "image_source": "images/page_14_equation_7.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.116,
            0.46,
            0.881,
            0.545
          ],
          "content": "which is of the form $y = m x + d .$ Here $m = - \\frac{a}{b} \\bmod d = - \\frac{c}{b}.$ Hence $y = mx + d$ is another form of expressing the linear equation $ax + by + c = 0$ , provided $b \\neq 0 .$ ."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.116,
            0.548,
            0.881,
            0.589
          ],
          "content": "Consider the line $5x + y = 3$ . Rewrite it as y = –5x + 3. Can you now find its slope? What does it mean?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.116,
            0.593,
            0.881,
            0.652
          ],
          "content": "In $y = - 5x + 3, m = - 5$ is the slope, which means for every increase of 1 unit in x, y decreases by 5 units. d = 3 is the y-intercept which means the line crosses the y-axis at (0, 3)."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.152,
            0.658,
            0.661,
            0.679
          ],
          "content": "Let us make a table of values to draw its graph."
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "bbox": [
            0.22,
            0.683,
            0.778,
            0.745
          ],
          "content": "| x | 0 | 1 | -1 |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| y | 3 | -2 | 8 |",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_14_table_12.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.116,
            0.757,
            0.88,
            0.799
          ],
          "content": "Plotting the ordered pairs A (0, 3), B (1, –2), C (–1, 8) and drawing the line through them we get the graph of $y = - 5x + 3$"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.135,
            0.817,
            0.399,
            0.84
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "Think and Reflect"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.133,
            0.857,
            0.852,
            0.895
          ],
          "content": "Can you use the points A, B and C to verify that the slope of this line is indeed –5?"
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
